IMPORT prelude

-- Evidence Act 1893 (Singapore) -- the rules of evidence an accused person most meets at trial: whether I must give evidence
-- (s 122(1), (3)); which questions about my past and my character may be put to me if I do (ss 122(4) to (8), 56(4)); when I
-- may refuse to answer for fear of incriminating myself, and what a compelled answer can be used for (s 134(4), (6)); which facts
-- I must prove (ss 103, 105 to 108); whether my statement is a confession and whether I can prove my own admission (ss 17, 21);
-- whether my character is relevant and when my bad character and previous convictions can be proved (ss 54 to 56); whether my
-- wife or husband can be made to, or may, disclose what I told them (s 124); whether my lawyer may disclose what passed between
-- us, and whether I can be made to (ss 128, 130, 131); and whether the Act requires more than one witness or a corroborated
-- accomplice (ss 135, 136).
--
-- SEAT: the accused person -- the individual charged with an offence and tried in a Singapore court, who may also be a party
-- to civil proceedings. Every decision is phrased as that person would ask it, on facts that person could state.
--
-- Provisions covered: ss 17, 21, 54, 55, 56, 103, 105, 106, 107, 108, 122(1), (3) to (8), 124 (with the "specified offence"
-- definition in s 124(3)), 128, 130(1), (2), 131(1), 134(4), (6), 135, 136.
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition (incorporating all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021, in operation 31 December
-- 2021), SSO informal consolidation "version in force from 9/3/2025", retrieved as "Current version as at 01 Oct 2026", as
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/EA1893.txt.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: relevancy of facts generally (ss 5 to 16, 32 to 53), proof of documents (ss 61 to 92), the parol evidence rule
-- (ss 93 to 102), the presumptions (ss 109 to 116A), estoppel (ss 117 to 119), the other privileges (ss 123, 125 to 127, 128A,
-- 129, 132, 133), examination of witnesses (ss 137 to 169) and bankers' books (ss 170 to 176). Whether a confession is
-- voluntary and so admissible is not in this Act: ss 24 to 30 were "[Repealed by Act 15 of 2010]" and the rule now sits in the
-- Criminal Procedure Code 2010, which is not encoded here. The standard of proof is not stated in this Act beyond the s 3
-- definitions of "proved" and is not encoded. The First Schedule's lists of "child abuse offence" and "sexual offence" are asked
-- as facts, not worked out from the Schedule; Penal Code terms ("domestic worker", "vulnerable person", Chapter 16) are asked as
-- facts.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Evidence Act 1893 -- the accused person`

-- The kind of proceedings I am in. The Act's own phrases: "criminal proceedings" (ss 55, 56, 122(2) to (8)); "civil
-- proceedings" and "the parties to the suit" (s 122(1)); "civil cases" (s 54).
DECLARE `Proceedings` IS ONE OF
    `criminal proceedings against me`
    `civil proceedings to which I am a party`

§§ `122(1), (3) -- must I give evidence`

-- s 122(1): "In all civil proceedings, the parties to the suit ... are competent witnesses."
-- s 122(3): "In any criminal proceedings, the accused is competent to give evidence on behalf of himself or herself or any person
-- jointly charged with him or her, but is not compellable to do so."
-- (competent = allowed to give evidence; compellable = can be made to.) Section 122(1) says nothing about whether a party to a
-- civil suit is compellable, so that case is answered as not covered.
DECLARE `Whether I can be made to testify` IS ONE OF
    `I am competent to give evidence but cannot be compelled to`
    `I am a competent witness, and whether I can be compelled is not covered by this encoding`

GIVEN `the proceedings` IS A `Proceedings`
GIVETH A `Whether I can be made to testify`
DECIDE `Can I be made to give evidence` IS
    CONSIDER `the proceedings`
    WHEN `criminal proceedings against me` THEN `I am competent to give evidence but cannot be compelled to`
    WHEN `civil proceedings to which I am a party` THEN `I am a competent witness, and whether I can be compelled is not covered by this encoding`

§§ `122(4) to (8), 56(4) -- questions about my past and my character`

-- s 122(4): "Where in any criminal proceedings the accused gives evidence, then, subject to this section and section 56, the
-- accused must not in cross-examination be asked, and if asked is not required to answer, any question tending to reveal to the
-- court -- (a) the fact that the accused has committed, or has been charged with or convicted or acquitted of, any offence other
-- than the offence charged; or (b) the fact that the accused is generally or in a particular respect a person of bad disposition
-- or reputation."
-- (5): not where evidence of that fact is admissible (by s 14 or 15, CPC s 265 or 266, or any other written law) "for the purpose
-- of proving the commission by him or her of the offence charged".
-- (6): where 2 or more are jointly charged, not where evidence of that fact "is admissible for the purpose of showing any other of
-- the accused to be not guilty".
-- (7): not if (a) I asked a prosecution or co-accused witness about the witness's conduct or offences, and (b) "the court is of
-- the opinion that the main purpose of that question was to raise an issue as to the witness's credibility"; "but the court is
-- not to permit a question falling within subsection (4) to be put to the accused by virtue of this subsection unless it is of
-- the opinion that the question is relevant to his or her credibility as a witness".
-- (8): not "where the accused has himself or herself given evidence against any person jointly charged".
-- s 56(4): where under s 56 a party is entitled to cross-examine me to show bad disposition or reputation -- which s 56(2) allows
-- once I have done any of the things in s 56(1) -- "section 122(4) does not apply".
-- The court's opinions are judgments the Act hands to the court: they are inputs here, never guessed.
DECLARE `What the question tends to reveal` IS ONE OF
    `another offence I committed, or was charged with, convicted or acquitted of`
    `that I am a person of bad disposition or reputation`
    `something else`

DECLARE `My cross-examination` HAS
    `I give evidence` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the question tends to reveal` IS A `What the question tends to reveal`
    `evidence of that fact is admissible to prove that I committed the offence charged` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am jointly charged and evidence of that fact is admissible to show a co-accused not guilty` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I asked a prosecution or co-accused witness about the witness's conduct or offences` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the court is of the opinion that the main purpose of my question was to raise the witness's credibility` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the court is of the opinion that the question to me is relevant to my credibility as a witness` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I gave evidence against a person jointly charged with me` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I did one of the things in section 56(1) to show my good disposition or reputation` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `What happens to the question` IS ONE OF
    `I am not cross-examined, because I have not given evidence`
    `section 122(4) does not reach this question`
    `it must not be asked, and if asked I need not answer it`
    `it may be asked`
    `the court is not to permit it`

GIVEN `the cross-examination` IS A `My cross-examination`
GIVETH A `What happens to the question`
DECIDE `Must I answer a question about my past or my character` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the cross-examination`'s `I give evidence`
            THEN `I am not cross-examined, because I have not given evidence`
        IF `the question is outside section 122(4)`
            THEN `section 122(4) does not reach this question`
        IF `subsection (5), (6) or (8), or section 56(4), lifts the protection`
            THEN `it may be asked`
        IF `subsection (7)(a) and (b) are met`
            THEN `what subsection (7) allows`
        OTHERWISE `it must not be asked, and if asked I need not answer it`
    WHERE
        `the question is outside section 122(4)` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the cross-examination`'s `the question tends to reveal`
            WHEN `something else` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE
        `subsection (5), (6) or (8), or section 56(4), lifts the protection` MEANS
                `the cross-examination`'s `evidence of that fact is admissible to prove that I committed the offence charged`
            OR  `the cross-examination`'s `I am jointly charged and evidence of that fact is admissible to show a co-accused not guilty`
            OR  `the cross-examination`'s `I gave evidence against a person jointly charged with me`
            OR  `the cross-examination`'s `I did one of the things in section 56(1) to show my good disposition or reputation`
        `subsection (7)(a) and (b) are met` MEANS
                `the cross-examination`'s `I asked a prosecution or co-accused witness about the witness's conduct or offences`
            AND `the cross-examination`'s `the court is of the opinion that the main purpose of my question was to raise the witness's credibility`
        `what subsection (7) allows` MEANS
            IF `the cross-examination`'s `the court is of the opinion that the question to me is relevant to my credibility as a witness`
                THEN `it may be asked`
                ELSE `the court is not to permit it`

§§ `134(4), (6) -- refusing to answer because it would incriminate me`

-- s 134(4): "Where the accused gives evidence in any criminal proceedings -- (a) the accused is not entitled to refuse to answer a
-- question or produce a document or thing on the ground that to do so would tend to prove the commission by him or her of the
-- offence charged; and (b) except as regards any question, document or thing which in the opinion of the court is relevant solely
-- or mainly to the accused's credibility as a witness (not being, in the case of a question, one asked by virtue of section 56),
-- the accused is not entitled to refuse ... on the ground that to do so would -- (i) tend to expose the accused to proceedings for
-- some other offence or for the recovery of a penalty; or (ii) tend to expose the accused's wife or husband to proceedings for an
-- offence or for the recovery of a penalty."
-- The exception in (b) only stops (b) from taking a right away; it does not grant one, and s 134(1) says generally that "A witness
-- is not excused from answering" on the ground of crimination. Whether a right to refuse survives in the excepted case is not
-- decided by the text read here, so that case is answered as not covered.
-- s 134(6): "No answer which an accused ... is compelled to give under subsection (4)(b) ... shall -- (a) expose the accused to any
-- proceedings for some other offence or for the recovery of a penalty or be proved against him or her in any such proceedings".
-- (6) speaks only of answers compelled under (4)(b): an answer compelled under (4)(a) is answered NOTHING (not covered).
DECLARE `Ground for refusing` IS ONE OF
    `it would tend to prove that I committed the offence charged`
    `it would tend to expose me to proceedings for another offence or for a penalty`
    `it would tend to expose my wife or husband to proceedings for an offence or for a penalty`

DECLARE `My refusal to answer` HAS
    `the ground` IS A `Ground for refusing`
    `the court is of the opinion that it is relevant solely or mainly to my credibility` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the question is asked by virtue of section 56` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Whether I may refuse` IS ONE OF
    `I am not entitled to refuse`
    `section 134(4) does not take away a right to refuse, and whether I have one is not covered by this encoding`

GIVEN `the refusal` IS A `My refusal to answer`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the credibility exception in section 134(4)(b) applies` IF
        `the refusal`'s `the court is of the opinion that it is relevant solely or mainly to my credibility`
    AND NOT `the refusal`'s `the question is asked by virtue of section 56`

GIVEN `the refusal` IS A `My refusal to answer`
GIVETH A `Whether I may refuse`
DECIDE `May I refuse to answer because it would incriminate me` IS
    CONSIDER `the refusal`'s `the ground`
    WHEN `it would tend to prove that I committed the offence charged` THEN `I am not entitled to refuse`
    OTHERWISE IF `the credibility exception in section 134(4)(b) applies` `the refusal`
                THEN `section 134(4) does not take away a right to refuse, and whether I have one is not covered by this encoding`
                ELSE `I am not entitled to refuse`

GIVEN `the refusal` IS A `My refusal to answer`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my compelled answer be proved against me in proceedings for another offence` IS
    CONSIDER `the refusal`'s `the ground`
    WHEN `it would tend to prove that I committed the offence charged` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE IF `the credibility exception in section 134(4)(b) applies` `the refusal`
                THEN NOTHING
                ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `103, 105 to 108 -- which facts I must prove`

-- s 103(1): "Whoever desires any court to give judgment as to any legal right or liability, dependent on the existence of facts
-- which the person asserts, must prove that those facts exist." Illustration (a): A, who wants B punished for a crime, "must prove
-- that B has committed the crime".
-- s 105: the burden as to a particular fact "lies on that person who wishes the court to believe in its existence". Illustration
-- (a): the prosecutor wishing the court to believe B admitted the theft "must prove the admission"; (b): "B wishes the court to
-- believe that at the time in question he or she was elsewhere. B must prove it."
-- s 106: the burden of proving a fact needed "to enable any person to give evidence of any other fact is on the person who wishes
-- to give such evidence".
-- s 107: "When a person is accused of any offence, the burden of proving the existence of circumstances bringing the case within
-- any of the general exceptions in the Penal Code 1871, or within any special exception or proviso contained in any other part of
-- the Penal Code 1871, or in any law defining the offence, is upon the person, and the court is to presume the absence of such
-- circumstances."
-- s 108: "When any fact is especially within the knowledge of any person, the burden of proving that fact is upon that person."
-- Illustration (b): a person charged with travelling on a railway without a ticket must prove he or she had a ticket.
DECLARE `Fact at my trial` IS ONE OF
    `that I committed the offence charged`
    `that I admitted the offence to someone`
    `circumstances bringing my case within a general exception in the Penal Code`
    `circumstances bringing my case within a special exception or proviso in the Penal Code or the law defining the offence`
    `that I was elsewhere at the time`
    `a fact especially within my knowledge`
    `a fact that must be proved before evidence I wish to give is admissible`

DECLARE `Burden` IS ONE OF
    `the burden is on the prosecution`
    `the burden is on me`
    `the burden is on me, and the court is to presume those circumstances absent`

GIVEN `the fact` IS A `Fact at my trial`
GIVETH A `Burden`
DECIDE `Must I prove this fact` IS
    CONSIDER `the fact`
    WHEN `that I committed the offence charged` THEN `the burden is on the prosecution`
    WHEN `that I admitted the offence to someone` THEN `the burden is on the prosecution`
    WHEN `circumstances bringing my case within a general exception in the Penal Code` THEN `the burden is on me, and the court is to presume those circumstances absent`
    WHEN `circumstances bringing my case within a special exception or proviso in the Penal Code or the law defining the offence` THEN `the burden is on me, and the court is to presume those circumstances absent`
    WHEN `that I was elsewhere at the time` THEN `the burden is on me`
    WHEN `a fact especially within my knowledge` THEN `the burden is on me`
    WHEN `a fact that must be proved before evidence I wish to give is admissible` THEN `the burden is on me`

§§ `17, 21 -- my statements, admissions and confessions`

-- s 17(1): "An admission is a statement, oral or documentary, which suggests any inference as to any fact in issue or relevant
-- fact, and which is made by any of the persons and under the circumstances hereinafter mentioned." (s 18(1): statements made by a
-- party to the proceeding are admissions; the accused is taken here to be such a party -- an inference, labelled.)
-- s 17(2): "A confession is an admission made at any time by a person accused of an offence, stating or suggesting the inference
-- that the person committed that offence."
-- s 21: admissions "may be proved as against the person who makes them ...; but they cannot be proved by or on behalf of the
-- person who makes them ... except" (a) where, if the maker were dead, it would be relevant between third persons under s 32;
-- (b) where it states a state of mind or body "made at or about the time when such state of mind or body existed and is
-- accompanied by conduct rendering its falsehood improbable"; (c) "if it is relevant otherwise than as an admission".
DECLARE `My statement` HAS
    `it suggests an inference as to a fact in issue or relevant fact` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am accused of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it states or suggests the inference that I committed that offence` IS A BOOLEAN
    `if I were dead it would be relevant between third persons under section 32` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it states my state of mind or body at or about the time, with conduct rendering its falsehood improbable` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is relevant otherwise than as an admission` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the statement` IS A `My statement`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my statement an admission` IF
    `the statement`'s `it suggests an inference as to a fact in issue or relevant fact`

GIVEN `the statement` IS A `My statement`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my statement a confession` IF
        `Is my statement an admission` `the statement`
    AND `the statement`'s `I am accused of the offence`
    AND `the statement`'s `it states or suggests the inference that I committed that offence`

GIVEN `the statement` IS A `My statement`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I prove my own admission in my favour` IF
        `the statement`'s `if I were dead it would be relevant between third persons under section 32`
    OR  `the statement`'s `it states my state of mind or body at or about the time, with conduct rendering its falsehood improbable`
    OR  `the statement`'s `it is relevant otherwise than as an admission`

§§ `54 to 56 -- my character`

-- s 54: "In civil cases the fact that the character of any person concerned is such as to render probable or improbable any
-- conduct imputed to him or her is irrelevant, except insofar as such character appears from facts otherwise relevant."
-- s 55: "In criminal proceedings, the fact that the person accused is of a good character is relevant."
-- s 56(1): the accused may (a) ask questions of any witness to establish that he or she is "a person of good disposition or
-- reputation", (b) give such evidence himself or herself, or (c) call a witness to give it. (2): "Where any of the things
-- mentioned in subsection (1) has been done, the prosecution may call, and any person jointly charged with the accused may call
-- or himself or herself give, evidence to establish that the accused is a person of bad disposition or reputation". (3): that
-- party "may call evidence of the accused's previous convictions, if any".
-- Explanation: "character" includes reputation and disposition; except as s 56 provides, only general reputation and general
-- disposition, "and not ... particular acts". Other routes to my bad character (ss 14, 15, s 122(5)) are not encoded.
DECLARE `My character evidence` HAS
    `the proceedings are` IS A `Proceedings`
    `my character appears from facts otherwise relevant` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I asked a witness questions to show my good disposition or reputation` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I gave evidence myself to show my good disposition or reputation` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I called a witness to show my good disposition or reputation` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Whether my bad character can be proved` IS ONE OF
    `yes, evidence of my bad disposition or reputation, and of my previous convictions`
    `not under section 56, which I have not opened`
    `section 56 applies only in criminal proceedings`

GIVEN `the evidence` IS A `My character evidence`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my character relevant to whether I did what I am accused of` IF
    CONSIDER `the evidence`'s `the proceedings are`
    WHEN `criminal proceedings against me` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `civil proceedings to which I am a party` THEN `the evidence`'s `my character appears from facts otherwise relevant`

GIVEN `the evidence` IS A `My character evidence`
GIVETH A `Whether my bad character can be proved`
DECIDE `Can my bad character be proved against me under section 56` IS
    CONSIDER `the evidence`'s `the proceedings are`
    WHEN `civil proceedings to which I am a party` THEN `section 56 applies only in criminal proceedings`
    WHEN `criminal proceedings against me` THEN
        IF      `the evidence`'s `I asked a witness questions to show my good disposition or reputation`
            OR  `the evidence`'s `I gave evidence myself to show my good disposition or reputation`
            OR  `the evidence`'s `I called a witness to show my good disposition or reputation`
        THEN `yes, evidence of my bad disposition or reputation, and of my previous convictions`
        ELSE `not under section 56, which I have not opened`

§§ `124 -- what I told my wife or husband`

-- s 124(1): "No person who is or has been married may be compelled to disclose any communication made to him or her during
-- marriage by any person to whom he or she is or has been married; nor may he or she be permitted to disclose any such
-- communication unless the person who made it or his or her representative in interest consents, except in suits between married
-- persons or proceedings in which one married person is prosecuted for any crime committed against the other."
-- (2): "Nothing in this section protects from disclosure any such communication made that is relevant in any criminal proceedings
-- in respect of a specified offence."
-- (3) "specified offence": (a) a child abuse offence against a person below 16; (b) a sexual offence or a Penal Code Chapter 16
-- offence against a person below 16; (c) a sexual offence or Chapter 16 offence against a domestic worker by the employer, a member
-- of the employer's household or an employment agent; (d) a sexual offence or Chapter 16 offence against a vulnerable person by a
-- person who has the custody, charge or care of the vulnerable person; (e) an abetment of, conspiracy to commit or attempt at any
-- of those (give the kind of the underlying offence).
-- Here I am the spouse who made the communication; I am taken to be the person charged with the offence. Whether the
-- communication is relevant in the proceedings is assumed, not asked.
-- The exception "except in suits between married persons or proceedings in which one married person is prosecuted for any crime
-- committed against the other" follows the permission limb; whether it also reaches the compulsion limb is not settled by the
-- grammar, so that case is answered as not covered.
DECLARE `Kind of offence` IS ONE OF
    `a child abuse offence`
    `a sexual offence`
    `an offence under Chapter 16 of the Penal Code that is not a sexual offence`
    `another offence`

DECLARE `The offence charged` HAS
    `the kind of offence` IS A `Kind of offence`
    `the victim was below 16 years of age` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the victim was a domestic worker and I was the employer, a member of the household or the employment agent` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the victim was a vulnerable person in my custody, charge or care` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence charged`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the offence charged a specified offence` IF
    CONSIDER `the offence`'s `the kind of offence`
    WHEN `a child abuse offence` THEN `the offence`'s `the victim was below 16 years of age`
    WHEN `another offence` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE
            `the offence`'s `the victim was below 16 years of age`
        OR  `the offence`'s `the victim was a domestic worker and I was the employer, a member of the household or the employment agent`
        OR  `the offence`'s `the victim was a vulnerable person in my custody, charge or care`

DECLARE `What I told my spouse` HAS
    `I told it to my spouse during our marriage` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I consent to its disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the proceedings are between my spouse and me, or one of us is prosecuted for a crime against the other` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the proceedings are criminal proceedings for` IS A MAYBE `The offence charged`

DECLARE `Whether my spouse can be compelled` IS ONE OF
    `section 124 does not protect it`
    `no, my wife or husband cannot be compelled to disclose it`
    `not covered by this encoding, because section 124(1) does not say whether its exception reaches the compulsion limb`

GIVEN `the communication` IS A `What I told my spouse`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 124(2) removes the protection` IF
    CONSIDER `the communication`'s `the proceedings are criminal proceedings for`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST `the offence` THEN `Is the offence charged a specified offence` `the offence`

GIVEN `the communication` IS A `What I told my spouse`
GIVETH A `Whether my spouse can be compelled`
DECIDE `Can my wife or husband be made to disclose what I told them` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the communication`'s `I told it to my spouse during our marriage`
            THEN `section 124 does not protect it`
        IF `section 124(2) removes the protection` `the communication`
            THEN `section 124 does not protect it`
        IF `the communication`'s `the proceedings are between my spouse and me, or one of us is prosecuted for a crime against the other`
            THEN `not covered by this encoding, because section 124(1) does not say whether its exception reaches the compulsion limb`
        OTHERWISE `no, my wife or husband cannot be compelled to disclose it`

GIVEN `the communication` IS A `What I told my spouse`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May my wife or husband disclose what I told them` IF
        NOT `the communication`'s `I told it to my spouse during our marriage`
    OR  `section 124(2) removes the protection` `the communication`
    OR  `the communication`'s `I consent to its disclosure`
    OR  `the communication`'s `the proceedings are between my spouse and me, or one of us is prosecuted for a crime against the other`

§§ `128, 130, 131 -- what passed between me and my lawyer`

-- s 128(1): "No advocate or solicitor is at any time permitted, unless with his or her client's express consent, to disclose any
-- communication made to him or her in the course and for the purpose of his or her employment ... or to state the contents or
-- condition of any document with which he or she has become acquainted in the course and for the purpose of his or her
-- professional employment, or to disclose any advice given by him or her to his or her client in the course and for the purpose
-- of such employment."
-- (2): "Nothing in this section protects from disclosure -- (a) any such communication made in furtherance of any illegal purpose;
-- (b) any fact observed by any advocate or solicitor in the course of his or her employment as such showing that any crime or
-- fraud has been committed since the commencement of his or her employment." Explanation: the obligation "continues after the
-- employment has ceased". Illustration (a): "I have committed forgery and I wish you to defend me" is protected; (b): a request to
-- sue on a forged deed is not; (c): an entry the solicitor sees made in the client's account book after the employment began is
-- not.
-- A fact the lawyer merely observed is not among the things s 128(1) protects (communications, the contents or condition of a
-- document, advice), so it is unprotected (an inference from the list, labelled).
-- s 130(1): a party who gives evidence "is not deemed to have consented thereby" to disclosure. (2): a party who calls the advocate
-- as a witness is deemed to consent "only if that party questions the advocate or solicitor on matters which but for the question
-- the advocate or solicitor would not be at liberty to disclose".
-- s 131(1): "No one may be compelled to disclose to the court any confidential communication which has taken place between him or
-- her and his or her legal professional adviser unless he or she offers himself or herself as a witness, in which case he or she
-- may be compelled to disclose any such communications as may appear to the court necessary to be known in order to explain any
-- evidence which he or she has given, but no others."
DECLARE `What my lawyer knows` IS ONE OF
    `a communication I made to my lawyer`
    `the contents or condition of a document`
    `advice my lawyer gave me`
    `a fact my lawyer observed`

DECLARE `Something my lawyer learned` HAS
    `what it is` IS A `What my lawyer knows`
    `it came to my lawyer in the course and for the purpose of the lawyer's employment as my advocate and solicitor` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I gave my express consent to its disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I called my lawyer as a witness and questioned the lawyer on it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it was made in furtherance of an illegal purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it shows that a crime or fraud has been committed since the lawyer's employment began` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I have given evidence myself` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the matter` IS A `Something my lawyer learned`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May my lawyer disclose it` IF
        `not among what section 128(1) protects`
    OR  `I consented, expressly or as section 130(2) deems`
    OR  `section 128(2) removes the protection`
    WHERE
        `not among what section 128(1) protects` MEANS
                NOT `the matter`'s `it came to my lawyer in the course and for the purpose of the lawyer's employment as my advocate and solicitor`
            OR  CONSIDER `the matter`'s `what it is`
                WHEN `a fact my lawyer observed` THEN TRUE
                OTHERWISE FALSE
        `I consented, expressly or as section 130(2) deems` MEANS
                `the matter`'s `I gave my express consent to its disclosure`
            OR  `the matter`'s `I called my lawyer as a witness and questioned the lawyer on it`
        `section 128(2) removes the protection` MEANS
                `(a) a communication in furtherance of an illegal purpose`
            OR  `the matter`'s `it shows that a crime or fraud has been committed since the lawyer's employment began`
        `(a) a communication in furtherance of an illegal purpose` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the matter`'s `what it is`
            WHEN `a communication I made to my lawyer` THEN `the matter`'s `it was made in furtherance of an illegal purpose`
            OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `I offered myself as a witness` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court considers it necessary to know to explain evidence I have given` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be made to disclose a confidential communication with my lawyer` IF
        `I offered myself as a witness`
    AND `the court considers it necessary to know to explain evidence I have given`

§§ `135, 136 -- how many witnesses`

-- s 135(1): "An accomplice is a competent witness against an accused person." (2): "Any rule of law or practice whereby at a trial
-- it is obligatory for the court to warn itself about convicting the accused on the uncorroborated testimony of an accomplice is
-- abrogated."
-- s 136: "No particular number of witnesses is in any case required for the proof of any fact."
-- So nothing in the Act requires a second witness or corroboration. Whether the one witness is believed is for the court and is
-- not encoded.
GIVEN `the number of witnesses to the fact` IS A NUMBER
      `the only witness is an accomplice whose testimony is uncorroborated` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act require more witnesses or corroboration before a fact is proved against me` IF
    FALSE
